=== layout: section

# Factorials

@notes

Source: lect07b-prob_of_progeny.odp, slide 11.

=== layout: two-panels

# *Factorial*

@left

- The factorial is the product of an integer and all the integers before it
  - factorial four, written as 4! is equal to\:
  - 4! = 1 &#215; 2 &#215; 3 &#215; 4 = 24.
- The number gets really big, really fast
- Short hand sometimes be used in the form\:
  - n! = n &#215; (n-1)!
  - 4! = 4 &#215; 3! = 4 &#215; 6 = 24

@right

| n | n! | ln(n!) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 | 0.0 |
| 1 | 1 | 0.0 |
| 2 | 2 | 0.7 |
| 3 | 6 | 1.8 |
| 4 | 24 | 3.2 |
| 5 | 120 | 4.8 |
| 6 | 720 | 6.6 |
| 7 | 5,040 | 8.5 |
| 8 | 40,320 | 10.6 |
| 9 | 362,880 | 12.8 |

@notes

Source: lect07b-prob_of_progeny.odp, slide 12.

=== layout: two-panels

# *Factorial*

@left

- The factorial is the product of an integer and all the integers before it
  - factorial four, written as 4! is equal to\:
  - 4! = 1 &#215; 2 &#215; 3 &#215; 4 = 24.
- [The number gets really big, really fast]{color=red}
- Short hand sometimes be used in the form\:
  - n! = n &#215; (n-1)!
  - 4! = 4 &#215; 3! = 4 &#215; 6 = 24

@right

| n | n! | ln(n!) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 6 | 1.8 |
| 4 | 24 | 3.2 |
| 5 | 120 | 4.8 |
| 6 | 720 | 6.6 |
| 7 | 5,040 | 8.5 |
| 8 | 40,320 | 10.6 |
| 9 | 362,880 | 12.8 |
| 10 | 3,628,800 | 15.1 |
| 11 | 39,916,800 | 17.5 |
| 12 | 479,001,600 | 20.0 |
| 13 | 6,227,020,800 | 22.6 |

@notes

Source: lect07b-prob_of_progeny.odp, slide 13.


