=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 8](assets/source_fidelity/lect07b/image_002.png)

- How many different ways can we get 4 boys and 3 girls?
- 35 ways out of 128 (2^7^) possible
- &#8594; 35/128 = 0.2734375 &#8776; 27.3%
- 4 girls and 3 boys the same 27.34%, these are mutually exclusive so&#8230;
- 4 boys and 3 girls or 4 girls and 3 boys is
- 27.3% + 27.3% &#8776; 54.7%

@notes

Source: lect07b-prob_of_progeny.odp, slide 25.

